    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP leans heavily into matchup exploitation today, headlined by **James Wood** (78.42) and **Mickey Moniak** (75.75). **James Wood** is our premier play, capitalizing on a soft matchup against Thornton (xERA 8.31); his base projection is bolstered by an elite 0.380 xwOBA and the leadoff spot, making him the anchor of our lineup. Similarly, **Mickey Moniak** benefits from a massive 14% boost due to dynamic platoon splits against Leiter, and he draws favorable park factors to maximize his damage potential. **Tyler Soderstrom** (75.27) rounds out our elite tier, taking advantage of a high-ceiling matchup against Kochanowicz, where the 19% SP skill adjustment highlights the exploitable nature of this start.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved **Austin Riley** (55.20), who narrowly missed the \"Strong\" start designation. Despite his pedigree, his lower batting order slot and suboptimal park factors dampened his efficiency score just enough to keep him on the fringes compared to the platoon-advantaged **Mickey Moniak** or **Tyler Soderstrom**. We continue to monitor the status of our depth, particularly with **Jose Caballero** and **Giancarlo Stanton** sidelined, forcing reliance on utility pieces like **Zach McKinstry**. Note that while we remain deep, our reliance on speed-heavy assets means **Zach McKinstry** remains a marginal start (46.99) largely due to the negative pressure from opposing elite catcher pop times, which tempers the value of his legs in our stolen base-focused 5x5 format. Ensure that as lineups finalize, any defensive substitutions do not compromise our projected volume for **Cole Young** or **Henry Bolte**, as both are currently tethered to favorable matchup scores that rely heavily on their confirmed batting order placement.",
